By the end of today’s class, you should be able to:

distinguish a mathematical variable from a programming variable;
explain the idea of program state;
work with basic Python types;
explain how integers and floating-point numbers are represented computationally;
recognize that computer arithmetic is performed using finite representations;
explain why floating-point calculations are approximate;
quantify numerical error using absolute and relative error;
compare floating-point numbers using appropriate tolerances;
describe a basic computational workflow:
problem → algorithm → implementation → testing → results

Throughout the class, we will use a recurring pattern:

Predict → Run → Inspect → Explain → Modify

